%I A396693 #72 Oct 23 2026 22:34:50
%S A396693 1,1,4,2,1352,1434378
%N A396693 a(n) is the number of order-n row-magic triangles formed by arranging the integers from 1 to n*(n+1)/2 exactly once in a triangular array, such that, for every k from 2 to n, the sums of the three corner-anchored triangular subarrays of side length k are equal, counted up to rotations and reflections.
%C A396693 Equivalently, the sums of the m-th rows counted from each of the three vertices are equal for every m from 3 to n, and the sums of the first two rows counted from each vertex are also equal.
%H A396693 Dairyong Jeong, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20070707181329/http://my.dreamwiz.com/dairyong/triple1.htm">Susja Nori: Original Row-Magic Triangles</a> (in Korean; archived Jul 07 2007).
%H A396693 Dairyong Jeong and Donghwi Park, <a href="https://gwahak.github.io/SusjaNori/pages/25fix.htm">Susja Nori: Row-Magic Triangles (revised version)</a> (in Korean).
%H A396693 Donghwi Park, <a href="https://github.com/gwahak/mathematics/blob/master/A396693/A396693_row_magic_triangle_all.ipynb">Source code for a(1) to a(6) and overview</a>.
%e A396693 a(1) = 1 and a(2) = 1 are trivial.
%e A396693 a(3) = 4. The four solutions, up to rotations and reflections, are:
%e A396693     1        1        4        2
%e A396693    6 4      6 5      3 2      5 3
%e A396693   3 2 5    2 4 3    5 1 6    4 1 6
%e A396693 For n = 3, the row-magic condition reduces to the perimeter-magic condition; hence a(3) = A342467(3) = 4.
%e A396693 a(4) = 2. The two complementary solutions, up to rotations and reflections, are:
%e A396693         1                 10
%e A396693       6  10              5   1
%e A396693     5   4   8          6   7   3
%e A396693   9   3   7   2      2   8   4   9
%Y A396693 Cf. A342467, A355119, A356643.
%K A396693 nonn,more,new
%O A396693 1,3
%A A396693 _Donghwi Park_, Oct 20 2026
